WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Translator: Ivana Korom
Reviewer: Krystian Aparta

00:00:13.532 --> 00:00:16.206
I'm here today to talk about fundraising,

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:18.809
or as you probably think of it,
"the other F-word."

00:00:18.833 --> 00:00:20.055
(Laughter)

00:00:20.079 --> 00:00:22.008
Because if you want to change the world,

00:00:22.032 --> 00:00:23.732
you have to know how to pay for it.

00:00:23.756 --> 00:00:25.804
I'm not talking
about being a good person --

00:00:25.828 --> 00:00:27.192
you can do that for free.

00:00:27.216 --> 00:00:29.978
I'm talking about if you want
to create something,

00:00:30.002 --> 00:00:31.303
start something,

00:00:31.327 --> 00:00:32.692
galvanize a community,

00:00:32.716 --> 00:00:35.192
improve the lives of others,
run for office.

00:00:35.979 --> 00:00:37.971
Every day, great ideas die on the vine,

00:00:37.995 --> 00:00:40.574
because they don't have capital
to get off the ground.

00:00:40.598 --> 00:00:42.058
And all of the work,

00:00:42.082 --> 00:00:44.558
the thought, the vision
that goes into the idea,

00:00:44.582 --> 00:00:46.744
isn't worth much
if you can't pay your bills.

00:00:47.088 --> 00:00:50.280
And while most of the greatest
social movements in history

00:00:50.304 --> 00:00:52.254
were powered purely by an idea

00:00:52.278 --> 00:00:54.556
and people's belief in that idea,

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:57.611
real change and impact require resources.

00:00:58.941 --> 00:01:01.727
Real people do this work,

00:01:01.751 --> 00:01:05.013
they need real change,
real impact and resources

00:01:05.037 --> 00:01:06.806
to actually make it happen.

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:08.688
The people that believe in this work

00:01:08.712 --> 00:01:11.895
have to have the support
and the resources to do it.

00:01:11.919 --> 00:01:13.125
That's where I come in.

00:01:13.149 --> 00:01:14.546
I get essential resources

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:17.911
into the hands of people
and visionaries on the front lines,

00:01:17.935 --> 00:01:19.085
doing work that matters.

00:01:19.704 --> 00:01:22.077
We spend the majority
of our waking hours working.

00:01:22.101 --> 00:01:25.299
We spend more time working
than we do with our loved ones.

00:01:25.323 --> 00:01:27.936
So I decided early on
that I have to love my work,

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:29.560
and it has to add value.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:32.531
And while I would love
to be one of these people

00:01:32.555 --> 00:01:36.674
who spearheads social change
from the ground up,

00:01:36.698 --> 00:01:39.712
the thing I realized early on
in my nonprofit career

00:01:39.736 --> 00:01:41.420
is that the thing I'm good at,

00:01:41.444 --> 00:01:43.285
the thing I'm really good at,

00:01:43.309 --> 00:01:44.527
is raising money.

00:01:45.196 --> 00:01:46.585
And I love it.

00:01:46.609 --> 00:01:48.109
I think it is a privilege

00:01:48.133 --> 00:01:51.817
to work alongside bold,
ambitious, optimistic leaders

00:01:51.841 --> 00:01:54.086
and the organizations they serve.

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:56.849
So I teach people
how to do the thing I'm good at,

00:01:56.873 --> 00:02:00.387
because the more people that learn
how to be good at my end of this work,

00:02:00.411 --> 00:02:02.298
the more work will get done.

00:02:02.322 --> 00:02:03.759
And I teach everyone.

00:02:03.783 --> 00:02:06.355
I teach CEOs and presidents,

00:02:06.379 --> 00:02:09.521
and boards of directors and EDs.

00:02:09.545 --> 00:02:12.702
I teach development directors
in all sorts of teams

00:02:12.726 --> 00:02:14.641
and nonprofit newbies,

00:02:14.665 --> 00:02:17.244
social change agents and candidates.

00:02:17.268 --> 00:02:19.879
I teach anyone that wants to do
something extraordinary

00:02:19.903 --> 00:02:21.696
how to fund their dream.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:24.069
My dream is that there will be
more people like me

00:02:24.093 --> 00:02:25.466
doing this work well

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:28.769
and that development will be
an undergraduate course at universities,

00:02:28.793 --> 00:02:33.118
so that fundraising animals like me
will find this job out of the gate,

00:02:33.142 --> 00:02:36.000
instead of discovering it
years later, accidentally.

00:02:36.024 --> 00:02:37.833
I even have the curriculum developed,

00:02:37.857 --> 00:02:41.345
but short of overhauling undergraduate
course requirements,

00:02:41.369 --> 00:02:43.488
I think tonight's probably
a good first step

00:02:43.512 --> 00:02:45.805
to get people to think about fundraising

00:02:45.829 --> 00:02:48.258
more as an opportunity

00:02:48.282 --> 00:02:50.170
and less as a dirty word.

00:02:50.953 --> 00:02:54.200
If you want to change the world,
you have to know how to pay for it.

00:02:54.224 --> 00:02:55.414
To do that well,

00:02:55.438 --> 00:02:57.414
you have to understand three big things.

00:02:57.438 --> 00:03:00.175
Your feelings about wealth and money,

00:03:00.199 --> 00:03:02.692
the importance of building relationships,

00:03:02.716 --> 00:03:04.916
and how to ask for what you want.

00:03:05.303 --> 00:03:08.296
Let's start at the top,
your feelings about wealth and money.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:10.907
What is your relationship to money?

00:03:10.931 --> 00:03:12.622
Money is complicated,

00:03:12.646 --> 00:03:14.204
it makes everyone squeamish,

00:03:14.228 --> 00:03:16.347
it makes everyone act kind of weird.

00:03:16.371 --> 00:03:19.586
Anyone who's ever had to split
the check after dinner with friends

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:20.815
can tell you this.

00:03:20.839 --> 00:03:23.101
Imagine what it was like before Venmo.

00:03:23.125 --> 00:03:24.807
(Laughter)

00:03:25.188 --> 00:03:27.149
To help people learn how to raise money,

00:03:27.173 --> 00:03:29.799
you have to help them understand
their deal with money,

00:03:29.823 --> 00:03:31.323
because everybody has baggage.

00:03:31.347 --> 00:03:32.990
Grew up poor? Baggage.

00:03:33.014 --> 00:03:34.480
Grew up rich? Baggage.

00:03:35.094 --> 00:03:38.483
Mad or envious that other people
have more money than you?

00:03:38.507 --> 00:03:39.657
Baggage.

00:03:39.681 --> 00:03:42.233
Think people with money
are smarter than you?

00:03:42.919 --> 00:03:44.070
Baggage.

00:03:44.094 --> 00:03:45.094
(Laughter)

00:03:45.118 --> 00:03:48.236
Feel guilty that you have
more money than other people?

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:50.116
That's some first-class baggage.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:52.022
(Laughter)

00:03:52.046 --> 00:03:54.621
It's still baggage, people,
it's still baggage.

00:03:54.645 --> 00:03:57.402
So whatever your deal is
with your baggage,

00:03:57.426 --> 00:03:58.645
you have to reconcile it

00:03:58.669 --> 00:04:00.732
if you're going to be able
to ask for money.

00:04:00.756 --> 00:04:03.295
And here's a little tip
about asking people for money.

00:04:03.319 --> 00:04:05.899
The only difference
about really wealthy people and us

00:04:05.923 --> 00:04:08.336
is that they have more money than us.

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:09.558
That's it.

00:04:09.582 --> 00:04:11.182
Don't overcomplicate it.

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:13.447
They come with their own baggage.

00:04:14.649 --> 00:04:17.522
When you think about how to do this work,

00:04:17.546 --> 00:04:20.589
it's important to remember
that money makes the world go round.

00:04:20.613 --> 00:04:22.641
You hear that all the time, but it's true.

00:04:22.665 --> 00:04:25.990
Whether you're a nonprofit, for-profit,
or you pay your own bills.

00:04:26.014 --> 00:04:30.422
We often feel like talking about it
is this icky, embarrassing, ugly thing,

00:04:30.446 --> 00:04:31.875
but it's just money.

00:04:31.899 --> 00:04:33.564
And it's a fact of life.

00:04:33.588 --> 00:04:36.962
So how you feel about it
directly affects how you approach it.

00:04:37.367 --> 00:04:40.262
Like everyone else
when I started out in this work,

00:04:40.286 --> 00:04:44.786
I had to examine and understand
my own feelings about wealth and money.

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:46.675
And I had to learn how to separate them

00:04:46.699 --> 00:04:49.762
from how I feel about raising money
for important causes.

00:04:50.077 --> 00:04:53.990
How I feel about asking for money
to help people do good work in the world

00:04:54.014 --> 00:04:57.323
is not the same as how I feel about
asking for money for myself.

00:04:57.649 --> 00:05:00.054
This is an important distinction.

00:05:00.078 --> 00:05:01.682
When I go and talk to someone,

00:05:01.706 --> 00:05:04.522
I'm not asking them to pay my mortgage.

00:05:04.546 --> 00:05:07.693
I'm giving them an opportunity
to invest in an idea

00:05:07.717 --> 00:05:10.045
that's going to change
the world for the better.

00:05:10.069 --> 00:05:12.269
Why should I feel bad about that?

00:05:12.879 --> 00:05:14.959
If you want to be good at raising money,

00:05:14.983 --> 00:05:16.902
you have to be able to reframe the ask,

00:05:16.926 --> 00:05:18.990
both for yourself and for other people,

00:05:19.014 --> 00:05:20.315
as an opportunity.

00:05:21.784 --> 00:05:24.645
Next, you have to get prepared
to build some relationships.

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:27.963
People give to people,
they don't just give to ideas.

00:05:27.987 --> 00:05:30.726
And if they don't believe
in the person running the place,

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:32.368
you're already dead in the water.

00:05:32.392 --> 00:05:36.416
This is true whether
you're in stocks or venture capital,

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:38.376
politics or nonprofits.

00:05:38.916 --> 00:05:41.498
Building a relationship
with people takes work.

00:05:42.138 --> 00:05:45.352
You have to care about more
than just what you want or need,

00:05:45.376 --> 00:05:48.043
you have to also value
what someone else wants or needs.

00:05:48.415 --> 00:05:50.597
I know, it's a shocking, terrible idea.

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:54.867
But oftentimes, closing gifts
is understanding the person,

00:05:54.891 --> 00:05:57.037
more than it's important
to know the product.

00:05:57.061 --> 00:06:00.585
And if you think building a relationship
with people takes work,

00:06:00.609 --> 00:06:03.927
building a relationship with someone
you're asking for money from

00:06:03.951 --> 00:06:06.786
takes work, and it takes homework.

00:06:07.347 --> 00:06:09.442
Have you done any research?

00:06:09.466 --> 00:06:11.767
Do you have any idea what they care about?

00:06:11.791 --> 00:06:14.045
Do you know why
they should invest in your work?

00:06:14.069 --> 00:06:16.752
Can you answer that question
in less than 30 seconds?

00:06:16.776 --> 00:06:19.728
If you can't, the meeting
is going to be pretty rough.

00:06:19.752 --> 00:06:22.195
And the answer can't be
"Because they're super rich

00:06:22.219 --> 00:06:23.770
and they live in your zip code."

00:06:25.477 --> 00:06:29.239
When you talk to people
and understand what they care about,

00:06:29.263 --> 00:06:30.850
it has to be in person.

00:06:30.874 --> 00:06:34.159
Fundraising is relational,
it's not transactional.

00:06:34.183 --> 00:06:36.516
And you have to ask them questions.

00:06:36.989 --> 00:06:40.108
When I sit down with a donor,
it goes something like this.

00:06:40.822 --> 00:06:42.704
"Hi, thanks so much for seeing me.

00:06:42.728 --> 00:06:43.917
How have you been?

00:06:43.941 --> 00:06:45.958
Did you guys go anywhere fun over holiday?

00:06:45.982 --> 00:06:47.141
Nice, I love Mexico.

00:06:47.165 --> 00:06:48.855
Do you always go to the same place?

00:06:48.879 --> 00:06:50.061
Oh, that's awesome!

00:06:50.085 --> 00:06:51.887
Are those your kids? They're so cute.

00:06:51.911 --> 00:06:53.061
How old are they?

00:06:53.085 --> 00:06:54.308
Where are they in school?

00:06:54.332 --> 00:06:57.180
Oh, that's a great school,
are you guys very involved there?

00:06:57.204 --> 00:06:59.141
Your spouse in on the board? How's that?

00:06:59.165 --> 00:07:00.356
How did you guys meet?

00:07:00.380 --> 00:07:02.117
Oh, at Santa Clara, that's awesome.

00:07:02.141 --> 00:07:04.268
Are you super involved
in the alumni network?

00:07:04.292 --> 00:07:05.503
So interesting.

00:07:05.527 --> 00:07:06.979
Where do you guys live, again?

00:07:07.003 --> 00:07:08.577
That's great. Is that your boat?"

00:07:08.601 --> 00:07:09.855
(Laughter)

00:07:09.879 --> 00:07:13.466
I literally go through
all of these things, right.

00:07:13.490 --> 00:07:14.641
And you know why?

00:07:14.665 --> 00:07:16.172
Because guess what I know now.

00:07:16.196 --> 00:07:19.712
I know they're out of 120 grand a year
in schooling for the next 12 years.

00:07:19.736 --> 00:07:20.902
Right?

00:07:20.926 --> 00:07:22.966
Spouse is on the board
of the kids' school,

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:24.791
I know they're out of 100K probably.

00:07:24.815 --> 00:07:26.014
It's a six-figure.

00:07:26.038 --> 00:07:28.156
They're both involved
in their school alumni,

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:29.363
that's probably 25K.

00:07:29.387 --> 00:07:31.637
They told me they live
on the Upper East Side --

00:07:31.661 --> 00:07:35.180
I can look up their apartment online
and find out what their mortgage is.

00:07:35.204 --> 00:07:37.259
And I know they own
a second home in Mexico.

00:07:37.283 --> 00:07:38.928
Oh, and they own a boat.

00:07:38.952 --> 00:07:41.071
Which is like funny money, right?

00:07:41.095 --> 00:07:43.659
So what I now understand --

00:07:43.683 --> 00:07:44.715
(Laughter)

00:07:44.739 --> 00:07:46.033
It's true.

00:07:46.057 --> 00:07:47.537
What I now understand

00:07:47.561 --> 00:07:51.591
is that their 1,000-dollar gift
is probably more of a starter gift.

00:07:51.615 --> 00:07:56.210
And I should be thinking about ways
to help them partner with us

00:07:56.234 --> 00:07:58.634
and invest in a more meaningful way.

00:07:59.234 --> 00:08:01.568
I know this sounds a tad mercenary.

00:08:01.982 --> 00:08:04.426
I'm not confused about how it sounds.

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:06.482
But here's what I want to tell you,

00:08:06.506 --> 00:08:09.654
because this is the part
that all my clients always want to skip,

00:08:09.678 --> 00:08:12.394
because they think it's the fluff
and it's not important.

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:16.260
If you don't understand
what they care about and what they value,

00:08:16.284 --> 00:08:19.577
how are you ever going to be able
to tell them about your work, right?

00:08:19.601 --> 00:08:22.006
I want them to fund our work, I do.

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:25.560
But I also want them to have
a really meaningful experience as a donor,

00:08:25.584 --> 00:08:27.720
so that they feel like we're partners

00:08:27.744 --> 00:08:30.188
and they're not an ATM, right?

00:08:30.212 --> 00:08:33.505
So it's important to ask the questions,

00:08:33.529 --> 00:08:35.252
because the more you know about them

00:08:35.276 --> 00:08:36.696
and you know what they value,

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:38.617
the more you can steer the conversation

00:08:38.641 --> 00:08:41.450
in a direction about your work
that will resonate for them.

00:08:41.474 --> 00:08:43.678
And once you get past
the get-to-know-you part,

00:08:43.702 --> 00:08:45.052
you get into the fun stuff,

00:08:45.076 --> 00:08:47.623
like, "Why are you philanthropic at all?"

00:08:47.647 --> 00:08:48.797
Right?

00:08:49.133 --> 00:08:51.529
"Why do you invest in new ideas?

00:08:51.553 --> 00:08:54.918
Do you want giving back to be a value
you pass on to your children?

00:08:54.942 --> 00:08:56.609
Can we help you do that?"

00:08:57.276 --> 00:08:59.078
It's really awesome, it's meaningful,

00:08:59.102 --> 00:09:00.792
and remember, it's a conversation,

00:09:00.816 --> 00:09:02.387
it's not a cross examination,

00:09:02.411 --> 00:09:03.877
it's not an interview.

00:09:04.744 --> 00:09:08.180
Don't walk in there and tell them
everything you already know about them,

00:09:08.204 --> 00:09:09.728
because you did your research.

00:09:09.752 --> 00:09:12.482
You don't get extra points
for knowing how to use Google.

00:09:12.506 --> 00:09:14.522
It's 75 percent them talking,

00:09:14.546 --> 00:09:16.314
25 percent you listening.

00:09:16.338 --> 00:09:19.029
It's better to be a good listener
than a good showman.

00:09:19.053 --> 00:09:21.942
And once you understand
what they care about,

00:09:21.966 --> 00:09:24.180
you can talk to them
about what you care about.

00:09:24.204 --> 00:09:25.713
You can tell them about you.

00:09:25.737 --> 00:09:27.593
Now, when you do this,

00:09:27.617 --> 00:09:30.250
don't get too deep into the weeds,
or you'll lose them.

00:09:30.274 --> 00:09:33.386
It's a lot like when I sit down
with guys in finance, right,

00:09:33.410 --> 00:09:34.560
and I say,

00:09:35.855 --> 00:09:37.022
you know, "How's work?"

00:09:37.046 --> 00:09:39.704
I'm looking for, like,
a thumbs up, thumbs down.

00:09:39.728 --> 00:09:42.683
But what I get sometimes

00:09:42.707 --> 00:09:49.684
is a long description
of how the markets are trending,

00:09:49.708 --> 00:09:51.823
and my brain leaves my body

00:09:51.847 --> 00:09:54.914
and starts to think about
what time my dry cleaner closes.

00:09:54.938 --> 00:09:55.953
(Laughter)

00:09:55.977 --> 00:09:57.935
Like, I don't have capacity for that.

00:09:57.959 --> 00:10:00.927
And they don't have capacity
for that level detail of our work.

00:10:00.951 --> 00:10:03.641
If they want it,
they'll ask you the questions.

00:10:03.665 --> 00:10:06.966
It's this thing that happens
over and over,

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:09.275
because -- here's an example.

00:10:09.665 --> 00:10:11.066
I worked with this CEO once,

00:10:11.090 --> 00:10:13.917
and I was hired to teach him
how to talk to human people,

00:10:13.941 --> 00:10:15.393
like a human person.

00:10:15.417 --> 00:10:16.418
(Laughter)

00:10:16.442 --> 00:10:18.639
It was a very difficult job.

00:10:18.663 --> 00:10:21.711
So, he kept getting great donor meetings,

00:10:21.735 --> 00:10:23.482
and he wasn't closing any gifts.

00:10:23.506 --> 00:10:25.768
And I could not figure out
what the problem was,

00:10:25.792 --> 00:10:28.418
so finally, I was like,
"I'm going to come with you."

00:10:28.442 --> 00:10:31.450
So I went with him to meetings,
and what would happen was,

00:10:31.474 --> 00:10:33.958
he was getting into such detail
with the donors

00:10:33.982 --> 00:10:36.117
that their eyes were glazing over,

00:10:36.141 --> 00:10:39.680
and then after he was done
with his 15-minute pitch,

00:10:39.704 --> 00:10:41.038
they literally would say --

00:10:41.062 --> 00:10:43.506
this happened, like,
three times in a row --

00:10:43.530 --> 00:10:45.950
"God, that sounds great. Congratulations.

00:10:46.617 --> 00:10:47.871
Keep up the good work."

00:10:47.895 --> 00:10:49.093
And that was the meeting,

00:10:49.117 --> 00:10:51.783
which was obviously not
the outcome we were looking for.

00:10:51.807 --> 00:10:54.593
So, he couldn't understand
what I was trying to say to him,

00:10:54.617 --> 00:10:57.474
that I finally, in an act
of sheer desperation, was like ...

00:10:58.998 --> 00:11:00.398
"You know what I love?

00:11:01.133 --> 00:11:02.283
I love NASA.

00:11:03.046 --> 00:11:04.696
I love NASA.

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:08.445
I think it is unbelievably amazing
we have figured out

00:11:08.469 --> 00:11:10.236
how to get a person to the Moon.

00:11:10.641 --> 00:11:11.974
I think it's awesome.

00:11:12.292 --> 00:11:15.170
I think the idea of getting
someone to the Moon,

00:11:15.194 --> 00:11:16.656
and they walk on the Moon,

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:18.887
and I love rocket ships.

00:11:18.911 --> 00:11:21.562
I love rocket ships,
rocket ships are amazing.

00:11:21.904 --> 00:11:24.253
But if you start to tell me
about the rocket ship,

00:11:24.277 --> 00:11:25.626
and how it gets to the Moon,

00:11:25.650 --> 00:11:27.518
and the math and the science equations,

00:11:27.542 --> 00:11:29.737
on how the rocket ship gets to the Moon,

00:11:29.761 --> 00:11:32.671
I promise you, I will hang myself
with my own hair."

00:11:32.695 --> 00:11:34.718
(Laughter)

00:11:35.028 --> 00:11:38.774
I was like, "That is not
how you tell people about your work.

00:11:38.798 --> 00:11:39.948
What is the need?"

00:11:40.655 --> 00:11:42.347
Like, what's the point, right?

00:11:42.371 --> 00:11:43.752
How do you address the need,

00:11:43.776 --> 00:11:45.783
why are you better at it
than anybody else?

00:11:45.807 --> 00:11:49.663
And what can you do to make it about them?

00:11:49.971 --> 00:11:53.074
How can they help you get to the Moon?

00:11:53.423 --> 00:11:54.890
That's the good stuff.

00:11:55.273 --> 00:11:58.518
If you're able to do that,
you're probably ready to make the ask.

00:11:58.542 --> 00:11:59.982
Now, I don't expect

00:12:00.006 --> 00:12:02.839
everyone to be super excited
to ask people for money.

00:12:02.863 --> 00:12:05.117
That's why development
is an actual profession

00:12:05.141 --> 00:12:06.788
and not an awkward hobby.

00:12:06.812 --> 00:12:07.822
(Laughter)

00:12:07.846 --> 00:12:11.323
Naturally great fundraisers love people,

00:12:11.347 --> 00:12:13.347
they can and will talk to anyone,

00:12:13.371 --> 00:12:15.743
they can find common ground with anyone,

00:12:15.767 --> 00:12:18.545
they're your friends
that talk to people in the elevator

00:12:18.569 --> 00:12:20.169
or at the grocery store.

00:12:20.791 --> 00:12:23.982
They believe in the work required

00:12:24.006 --> 00:12:26.584
to both build relationships and keep them.

00:12:27.007 --> 00:12:30.976
And they naturally have
a high tolerance for rejection.

00:12:31.975 --> 00:12:34.031
But I don't expect everyone
to be a natural,

00:12:34.055 --> 00:12:36.402
and you don't have to be
a natural to raise money.

00:12:36.426 --> 00:12:39.315
You just have to respect
the people and the process,

00:12:39.339 --> 00:12:40.489
and do the work.

00:12:41.307 --> 00:12:45.045
Will you reconcile your baggage?

00:12:45.403 --> 00:12:47.958
Will you commit to build relationships?

00:12:47.982 --> 00:12:50.450
If you will, you're ready to make the ask.

00:12:50.474 --> 00:12:53.323
And the ask is oftentimes
as simple as using the phrase

00:12:53.347 --> 00:12:54.681
"Would you consider?"

00:12:55.584 --> 00:12:57.989
Would you consider
becoming a monthly donor?

00:12:58.013 --> 00:13:01.213
Would you consider increasing
your support to 100 dollars?

00:13:01.554 --> 00:13:05.078
Would you consider investing in our work
at the one-million-dollar level?

00:13:05.419 --> 00:13:07.959
"Would you consider"
does a couple of awesome things.

00:13:07.983 --> 00:13:10.371
One, it gives the donor an easy way out.

00:13:10.395 --> 00:13:12.903
Like, they can say "no"
without it being "yes-no."

00:13:12.927 --> 00:13:14.958
And two, it gives you a second ask.

00:13:14.982 --> 00:13:16.545
"Well, what would you consider?"

00:13:16.569 --> 00:13:18.618
(Laughter)

00:13:18.642 --> 00:13:19.793
It's good, right?

00:13:19.817 --> 00:13:20.967
(Laughter)

00:13:21.491 --> 00:13:24.823
When you do this, remember,
you're not asking for yourself.

00:13:24.847 --> 00:13:27.610
You're asking on behalf
of all of the people you serve

00:13:27.634 --> 00:13:29.884
or are touched by your genius.

00:13:29.908 --> 00:13:32.154
This isn't a personal favor, right?

00:13:32.178 --> 00:13:35.945
Feel proud of the ask --
it's incredible that you do this work.

00:13:36.836 --> 00:13:38.527
Don't try to be someone you're not,

00:13:38.551 --> 00:13:41.893
you're going to go to these meetings
and think you need to big-shot it.

00:13:41.917 --> 00:13:44.576
Be yourself, authenticity matters,
nobody likes a phony.

00:13:44.600 --> 00:13:45.838
Just be yourself.

00:13:45.862 --> 00:13:47.361
And please,

00:13:47.385 --> 00:13:49.652
please don't torpedo your own ask.

00:13:50.092 --> 00:13:51.290
What I mean by this

00:13:51.314 --> 00:13:55.585
is don't walk into the meeting and say --
I had an ED that did this all the time,

00:13:55.609 --> 00:13:57.276
I stopped inviting him.

00:13:57.688 --> 00:14:00.235
He'd say, "We're not here today
to ask you for money."

00:14:00.259 --> 00:14:02.537
(Laughter)

00:14:02.561 --> 00:14:03.718
Yes, we are!

00:14:03.742 --> 00:14:04.743
(Laughter)

00:14:04.767 --> 00:14:08.751
That's exactly, literally,
why we're here today.

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:10.330
Don't do that.

00:14:10.728 --> 00:14:13.009
Don't say, "Whatever you can do to help."

00:14:13.402 --> 00:14:17.251
That is hands down the fastest way
to get the smallest possible gift

00:14:17.275 --> 00:14:19.704
someone thinks they can give you
and get away with.

00:14:19.728 --> 00:14:20.886
Not kidding.

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:23.093
And don't take it back.

00:14:23.117 --> 00:14:24.455
Once you've made the ask --

00:14:24.479 --> 00:14:27.641
"Would you consider supporting us
at the 10,000-dollar level?

00:14:27.665 --> 00:14:29.483
Or the five? Or the two? Or one?

00:14:29.507 --> 00:14:31.109
You know what? Take the year off.

00:14:31.133 --> 00:14:32.379
You're the best, thanks!"

00:14:32.403 --> 00:14:33.664
Don't do that!

00:14:34.593 --> 00:14:36.260
Ask the question,

00:14:36.284 --> 00:14:39.380
wait till 10, count to 10
before you speak again,

00:14:39.404 --> 00:14:41.071
keep your face like this.

00:14:41.800 --> 00:14:43.680
(Laughter)

00:14:43.704 --> 00:14:45.389
They are grown-ups.

00:14:46.411 --> 00:14:48.871
They have all the power in this situation.

00:14:48.895 --> 00:14:50.625
They can answer the question.

00:14:50.649 --> 00:14:51.915
Don't take it back.

00:14:52.331 --> 00:14:54.085
Which brings me to my favorite.

00:14:54.109 --> 00:14:55.958
Don't ask, don't get.

00:14:55.982 --> 00:14:57.887
If you don't make an actual ask,

00:14:57.911 --> 00:14:59.814
no one will give you actual money.

00:14:59.838 --> 00:15:01.601
And if no one gives you actual money,

00:15:01.625 --> 00:15:03.593
you actually can't do anything with it.

00:15:03.617 --> 00:15:06.217
It's very simple -- don't ask, don't get.

00:15:07.784 --> 00:15:09.657
Listen, I would love to live in a world

00:15:09.681 --> 00:15:11.800
where we didn't have
to ask people for money

00:15:11.824 --> 00:15:15.125
to do important work
that will change people's lives.

00:15:15.149 --> 00:15:17.363
I would love to not have to teach people

00:15:17.387 --> 00:15:20.617
how to make a case for the importance
of feeding and housing

00:15:20.641 --> 00:15:22.656
and educating people.

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:24.506
But this is the world that we live in,

00:15:24.530 --> 00:15:26.561
and if we're committed to doing this work,

00:15:26.585 --> 00:15:27.768
and doing it well,

00:15:27.792 --> 00:15:30.561
we have to be as committed
to the art of funding this work

00:15:30.585 --> 00:15:32.863
as we are to the art of executing it.

00:15:32.887 --> 00:15:35.911
I'm going to repeat that,
because I think it's really important.

00:15:35.935 --> 00:15:39.588
We have to be as committed
to the art of funding our work

00:15:39.612 --> 00:15:41.946
as we are to the art of executing it.

00:15:42.771 --> 00:15:43.929
And at its core,

00:15:43.953 --> 00:15:47.144
the art of funding the work
means that we have to truly believe

00:15:47.168 --> 00:15:50.117
that the purpose
and the privilege of our work

00:15:50.141 --> 00:15:51.799
is to provide people

00:15:51.823 --> 00:15:54.479
with an extraordinary way
to use their wealth

00:15:54.503 --> 00:15:56.636
that will change people's lives.

00:15:57.286 --> 00:15:59.079
It's an opportunity,

00:15:59.103 --> 00:16:01.166
because at its core, that's what it is.

00:16:01.190 --> 00:16:02.657
And how great is that?

00:16:03.413 --> 00:16:04.571
Thank you.

00:16:04.595 --> 00:16:07.134
(Applause, cheers)

